  <section>
    <h2>What to do with a swollen laptop or phone battery</h2>
    <p>A swollen lithium battery — visible as the phone case popping outward, laptop keyboard rising up, or battery pack physically bulging — is a real fire and burn risk if punctured, crushed, or shorted. Safe sequence:</p>
    <p>Leave the battery inside the device. Do not try to remove a swollen laptop or phone battery yourself.</p>
    <p>Place the device on a non-flammable surface — ceramic tile, glass, concrete. Not on paper, carpet, wood, plastic.</p>
    <p>Keep away from direct sunlight, heat sources, and enclosed metal containers (which could short the battery across the case).</p>
    <p>Take a photo showing the swelling.</p>
    <p>Message the team with the photo and 'swollen battery' flag. They bring appropriate containment (thermally-stable containers) for damaged battery pickup.</p>
    <p>Do not: puncture, crush, bend, take apart, submerge in water, or throw into regular trash. Do not put a swollen battery into your car's boot without proper containment.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Why different battery types route to different recycling streams</h2>
    <p>Battery chemistry determines the recycling infrastructure. In India:</p>
    <p>Lead-acid batteries (UPS, inverter, car, two-wheeler) have well-established recycling. Lead is recovered for reuse in new batteries; acid is neutralised. This is a mature industry with regulated recyclers.</p>
    <p>Lithium-ion batteries (laptop, phone, power bank, e-vehicle) route through a more recent recycling infrastructure. Cobalt, nickel, lithium and copper are recovered depending on cell chemistry. Growing capacity in India.</p>
    <p>Alkaline and button cells (remote batteries, watch batteries, hearing aid batteries) route through specialised small-cell recycling for the mercury and heavy metals some contain.</p>
    <p>None of this happens by dropping mixed batteries into general recycling — the chemistry-specific streams need chemistry-specific input. Battery pickup routes each type to the correct stream.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Storing batteries before pickup</h2>
    <p>For batteries in working or worn condition (no damage), storage is easy:</p>
    <p>Cool, dry place — normal cupboard or shelf is fine. Not in direct sunlight, not in a hot balcony or window sill.</p>
    <p>Away from other metals — a drawer full of old batteries plus a metal spoon or coin can create a short circuit across a battery's terminals. Keep batteries separated from loose metal objects.</p>
    <p>Don't stack heavy items on top — pressure can damage cells over time.</p>
    <p>For batteries showing any damage — swelling, leakage, corrosion, unusual heat — the storage rules tighten: non-flammable surface, extra space around the battery, no combustible materials nearby, and get the pickup booked quickly rather than storing for weeks.</p>
  </section>

